course: dictionary
lesson: 4002
title: Old spellings and old jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words look strange because they are old spellings. Gaoler is an old British way of spelling jailer. When you meet a word you do not know, sound it out and look for a familiar word that sounds similar.
explain: «The gaoler's kind daughter, who loves animals, asks to look after him.» The sentence tells you a gaoler has a daughter and that someone is kept locked up. Put the clues together and a gaoler must be a person who guards prisoners.
explain: Check by swapping in your meaning: «The prison officer's kind daughter asks to look after him.» It still makes sense, so gaoler and prison officer mean much the same. Today we would say prison officer.
fact: Gaoler is the old British spelling of jailer.
fact: A gaoler is a person whose job is to guard prisoners in a prison.
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, Toad is locked in a dungeon guarded by an old gaoler.
fact: Old books often use old spellings, so a word can look new to you even when you know what it means.
exercise: «The old gaoler's rusty key creaks, and the door clangs.» What is a gaoler? | A kind washerwoman | A person who sells keys | *A person who guards prisoners | A type of heavy door
exercise: In the story, Toad is locked in a dungeon guarded by an old gaoler. Who does the gaoler watch over? | Barge-women | Trees and birds | *Prisoners | Engine-drivers
exercise: Which word is another spelling of gaoler? | Goalie | *Jailer | Gargoyle | Gallery
test: Today we would say «prison officer» for a gaoler. What is a gaoler's job? | Washing clothes | Cooking dinner for the family | Driving a train | *Guarding prisoners
test: «Toad sent the gaoler's daughter a handsome gold chain.» Is the gaoler's daughter a prisoner? | Yes, she is locked in a dungeon | *No, she is the daughter of the person who guards prisoners | Yes, she is a barge-woman | There are not enough clues to know
exam: Which clue best shows what a gaoler does? | *The gaoler's rusty key creaks and the door clangs shut on the prisoner | Toad sends a gold chain to the daughter | The engine-driver is properly thanked | The barge-woman is paid for her horse
